cartridge "NAME THAT STAR/BOB LLORET/1984"

' NAME THAT STAR
' A ColecoVision game based on "Name That Star" V2.0 by Bob Lloret, Ahoy!
' April 1984 (Commodore 64 BASIC, disk ahoy1984-jan-jun, orig/).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a TV game show. Fill in the contestant's application (your
' first name), and the host welcomes you and offers to explain the game.
' Forty mystery names of famous people follow, one at a time. The name is
' shown jumbled on the marquee and the host gives a clue in his speech
' balloon. You get three guesses at each name; after the third wrong
' guess the host tells you who it was. At the end the host says goodbye
' and tells you how many stars you named.
'
' From the original (orig/name that star.bas): the application, the
' acceptance, the host's dialogue (welcome, "let's welcome our player",
' the offer to explain, the four explanation pages, "if you're ready to
' go... let's play Name That Star", "our first / next mystery name is",
' "correct, you now have n correct", "wrong, you have 2 guesses left",
' "wrong again", "I'm sorry, but this famous person is", the closing
' lines), the 40 mystery names with their jumbled forms and clues, three
' guesses a name, the count of names guessed, the rising jingle on a
' correct answer, the light-bulb marquee and the speech balloon.
'
' Liberties:
' - Typed answers become joystick choices: six names (the right one and
'   five other stars of the 40, drawn at random) under the clue; a wrong
'   choice is crossed out and the pointer moves on. With choices, three
'   guesses would be too easy, so a guess also scores points: 3 for the
'   first guess, 2 for the second, 1 for the third (120 at most); the
'   original's count of names guessed is kept beside it. Random guessing
'   averages about 40 points.
' - The first name is spelled on a letter board (up to 8 letters).
' - The 40 names come in a new random order each game (the original
'   always asked them in the same order); the best score is kept on the
'   title.
' - "Type C to continue" is a press of a button; the delay loops are
'   short pauses or a press.
' - Facts corrected (stars.py lists each): Brando's clue (his roles, not
'   his heritage: he was not Italian), Gracie Allen (her character was
'   the scatterbrain), Chaplin (baggy pants, not tails), Oliver Hardy (a
'   film comic, not TV), Harrison Ford (Harrison is not a state: his
'   hero was named Indiana; WOOKIEE), Bill Bixby (David Banner turned
'   into the Hulk), Bob Denver (the beatnik and the castaway were two
'   roles), Leonard Nimoy (the nerve pinch knocks out, not paralyzes),
'   Clint Eastwood (Clyde was an orangutan, not a monkey), Boris Karloff
'   (not the first screen Frankenstein monster: Charles Ogle, 1910), and
'   the jumble of Danny Thomas (YANDY had a Y for an N).
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick up/down chooses, a button picks. On
' the letter board the stick moves in four directions; DEL rubs out a
' letter, END finishes. On the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` is given after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers. The marquee bulbs change once a
' second (nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz).

